Schema: Defines the template rules that Azure deployment has to follow such as JSON format, syntax, structure  
        and version. The "contentVersion" is a user-defined string that helps track template changes. Not used
        by Azure, but will be used by the team for version control. Schema versions "2019-04-
        01" are key to unlocking certain features.    
Example:  
{ 
"$schema": "https://schema.management.azure.com/schemas/2019-04-
01/deploymentTemplate. json#"
"contentVersion": "1.0.0.0"
}  

Parameters: Allow dynamic input, or acts as place holders, values, durning deployment, for reusability.  
            NO more Hard coded values!!! Example has two parameters, "storageAccountName" and "location".  
Example:    

"parameters": {
  "storageAccountName": {
     "type": "string",
     "metadata": {
       "description": "If the user do not add storage account name during deployment, then the default value 
                       name for the storage account will be created or used."
    },
    "defaultValue": "myStorageAccount"
}
"location": {
    "type": "string",
    "defaultValue": "[resourceGroup() .location]",
    "metadata": {
       "description": "Will use function "resourceGroup()" to pull the location of that resource Group and store 
                       that resource Group location value to the deployed resources created in different templates."  
    }
   }
  }


Variables: Computed values for simplicity. Reuse the same logic, complex expressions, by storing that logic in a variable, 
           then using that variable throughout the template. Define them based on parameters, other variable, or functions.
Example: 

"variables": {
'storageAccountType": "Standard_LRS"
"storageAccountNamePrefix": "[parameters('storageAccountNamePrefix' ) ]",
"storageAccountName" : "[concat(variables('storageAccountNamePrefix' ), 'sa')]",
"uniqueId" : "[uniqueString(resourceGroup().id]"
}

Functions: Provide built-in capabilities on internal logic for data manipulate.
For the variable "fullResourceName", it is using three functions such as toLower(one argument),  
parameters(one argument), and concat(argument 1, argument 2)
Example:  

"variables": {
"fullResourceName": "[toLower (concat(parameters(' storageAccountName'), '-rg')) ]",
"newNumber": "[add(parameters(' first'), parameters('second' ) ) ]",
"arrayElement": "[first(parameters('arrayToTest' )) ]"
}

User-Defined Functions: Customized functions that are not built-in to ARMs deployment.  
The namespace is what calls the billing UDF for any function. ex: billing.toUpper().
Rules for UDF: A UDF requires a dedicated namespace. A UDF cannot have access a variable or other UDF,  
which means we cannot reference a variable defined elsewhere in the template from within the UDF.  
Also, UDF can only access parameters defined within the scoped of that UDF, which means that UDF 
cannot use parameters or parts defined in the main template. 
Example:  

"functions": [
  {
    "namespace": "billing",
    "members": {
      "uniqueName": {
        "parameters": [ {
          "name": "namePrefix",
          "type": "string"
          } ],
        "output": {
          "type": "string",
          "value": "[concat(toLower (parameters('namePrefix')),  
uniqueString(resourceGroup().id))]"
} } } } ],

Resources: Declare the infrastructure to be deployed by defining the actual Azure Resource Provider.
resources[] is deployed as a array object, even if their is only one resource declared.
Example:  

"resources": [{ <---array start
  {
    "type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts",
    "apiVersion": "2024-01-01",
    "name": "[parameters('storageAccountName')]",
    "location": "[parameters('location')]",
    "sku": {
      "name": "Standard_LRS"
    },
    "kind": "StorageV2",
    "properties": {
      "supportsHttpsTrafficOnly": true
    }
  }] <---array end

Outputs: Return deployment values, such as public IP addresses or URLs, for further use. Very handy  
         for passing values or sharing information to other tools, scripts, or environments after deployments.
         Outputs let you return specific details about the reources that were deployed such as from our storage  
         account that we deployed we want the storageAccountUri value. And resourceIds wants a list of   
         VM resourceID values.
Example:  

"outputs": {
  "storageAccountUri": {
    "type": "string",
    "value":
"[reference('Microsoft. Storage/storageAccounts/account') .primaryEndpoints.blob]"
  },
  "resourceIds": {
    "type": "array",
    "value": "[concat(array(parameters('vmNames')), resourceID())]"
  }
}
